\section{Measure Joins Before Changing the Language}

Suppose a compiler needs a complete may-call closure as an input to several passes, so a query-specific slice is not enough. The immediate choice is not between imperative and declarative analysis. It is between joining yesterday's tuples again and joining only because a new tuple arrived. Holm's Scheme text develops a goal-oriented engine using unification and backtracking; here we optimize a different execution model, a bottom-up engine over function-free relations. Jordan, Scholz, and Suboti\'c describe how Souffl\'e specializes evaluation machinery into generated analysis code \cite{jordan2016souffle}. A rule author need not dictate every index, but the compilation of rules to physical joins determines much of the runtime.

For a toy five-function input, use positive Datalog with uppercase variables and lowercase constants. This intentionally uses a nonlinear rule with two recursive body occurrences; both can contribute fresh tuples.

\begin{verbatim}
edge(a,b). edge(a,c). edge(b,d). edge(c,d). edge(d,e).

path(X,Y) :- edge(X,Y).
path(X,Z) :- path(X,Y), path(Y,Z).
\end{verbatim}

There are nine final \texttt{path} rows: the five edges and \verb|path(a,d)|, \verb|path(b,e)|, \verb|path(c,e)|, \verb|path(a,e)|. The two derivations of \verb|path(a,d)| are not two rows. In the first composition round, four matching body assignments yield three new tuples. In round two, three joins involving a fresh row lead to \verb|path(a,e)|: through \(b\), through \(c\), and through \(d\). That is one distinct new tuple. In round three it has no matching extension or predecessor, so the delta is empty.

\section{Compile a Delta Rule for Each Recursive Position}

Let \(R\) be the relation known at the start of a round and \(\Delta\subseteq R\) the rows first seen in the preceding round (initially the seed edges). The old subset \(R_{\mathrm{old}}=R\setminus\Delta\) has already participated in all-old combinations. For the two recursive occurrences, compute \(\Delta\circ R\) and \(R_{\mathrm{old}}\circ\Delta\), where \(\circ\) is relational composition on the shared intermediate function. The first case includes matches in which both rows are fresh; the second does not repeat them. At least one row is new in every match. Deduplication is still needed since different intermediate functions may produce the same output pair.

