\section{Check the Same Answers With Less Repeated Work}

\begin{verbatim}
old := empty; delta := unique(edge)
while delta is nonempty:
    all := old union delta
    first  := join(delta, all, shared_middle)
    second := join(old, delta, shared_middle)
    next := unique(project_endpoints(first union second)) - all
    # Publish both tables together; do not expose a partial round.
    old := all; delta := next
return old
\end{verbatim}

Count successful body bindings before projecting and deduplicating, not failed index probes. In this example the naive all--all rounds match \(4,7,7\) bindings; the delta rounds match \(4,3,0\). The values give a small regression oracle, not a speedup claim. Remove \verb|edge(c,d).| and recompute both algorithms: \verb|path(c,e)| disappears along with the edge's own path row, but \verb|path(a,d)| and \verb|path(a,e)| still have proofs via \(b\). Deduplicating proofs during insertion does not preserve enough information to implement arbitrary deletion. For an engine without verified dynamic maintenance, discard the affected closure and rebuild it.

For indexing the first join, scan fresh \((X,Y)\) rows and look up right tuples by their first column \(Y\). For the second, scan fresh \((Y,Z)\) rows and look up old left tuples by their second column \(Y\). This trades extra indexes and update costs for cheaper probes. Build recursive predicate groups from strongly connected dependencies and evaluate nonrecursive producers once before their consumers. Measure relation cardinalities, probe counts, delta sizes, and index memory separately; a dense closure can be expensive even with an optimal join order. PQL is a useful contrast: its authors report using the bddbddb deductive database with binary decision diagrams. That does not imply it stores sparse tuple indexes like the engine sketched here. Keep the logical answers fixed while comparing representations and plans.

A compiler analysis should expose the completed \texttt{path} relation, not timing-sensitive intermediate deltas. This permits physical-plan experiments to optimize join work without changing the call-closure result consumed by later passes.
